I hear leaves drinking rain;
I hear rich leaves on top
Giving the poor beneath
Drop after drop;
'Tis a sweet noise to hear
These green leaves drinking near.
And when the Sun comes out,
After this Rain shall stop,
A wondrous Light will fill
Each dark, round drop;
I hope the Sun shines bright;
'Twill be a lovely sight.

Brightly hot the sun shines down on some lovely summer days
while the golden moon smiles romantically during long nights,
where are we with dawn or dusk when twilight divides the two?

J-ust let the twilight pass;
H-aze and mist fade away;
U-nderneath the blue sky,
N-ew dawn meets the day.
M-orn nineteenth February
I-s setting Sunday aright;
L-et the sunlight rise,
A-fter the long cold night.
N-ow that your daughter has finished primary education;
O-pen your arms to be an escort in graduation.

What a wonderful bird the frog are!
When he stands he sit almost;
When he hop he fly almost.
He ain't got no sense hardly;
He ain't got no tail hardly either.
When he sit, he sits on what he ain't got almost.

Where far in forest I am laid,
In a place ringed around by stones,
Look for no melancholy shade,
And have no thoughts of buried bones;
For I am bodiless and bright,
And fill this glade with sudden glow;
The leaves are washed in under-light;
Shade lies upon the boughs like snow.

G-ood morning sunshine
L-ets the dawn break with glee;
A-iming to make a beautiful
D-ay of mirth and ecstasy.
Y-ear added to the lady is something to remember;
S-adness should step back to make her feel glad and better.
M-oonlight shadows have faded,
A-fter the night walks away;
E-very twenty-second January, she celebrates her birthday.

The year's at the spring,
And day's at the morn;
Morning's at seven;
The hill-side's dew-pearl'd;
The lark's on the wing;
The snail's on the thorn;
God's in His heaven--
All's right with the world!

THOUGH leaves are many, the root is one;
Through all the lying days of my youth
I swayed my leaves and flowers in the sun;
Now I may wither into the truth.

Dance little baby, dance up high,
Never mind baby, mother is by;
Crow and caper, caper and crow,
There little baby, there you go;
Up to the ceiling, down to the ground,
Backwards and forwards, round and round;
Dance little baby, and mother shall sing,
With the merry coral, ding, ding, ding.

May your year be full of joy;
May laughter fill your days;
May morning's light bring fresh delight
Expelling dreary haze;
May friends and loved ones ever buoy
A heart weighed down by strife;
And may serenity alight
From the simple things in life.

Hope is like a harebell trembling from its birth,
Love is like a rose the joy of all the earth;
Faith is like a lily lifted high and white,
Love is like a lovely rose the world's delight;
Harebells and sweet lilies show a thornless growth,
But the rose with all its thorns excels them both.

Health is the first good lent to men;
A gentle disposition then:
Next, to be rich by no by-ways;
Lastly, with friends t' enjoy our days.

The heart so loves to love,
loves love, beyond the loving all;
The mind so plays, so loves to play,
plays with that greater play
of love, that plays with mind and time;
time plays with love, times out of mind,
but love’s the heart of it;
love loves beyond time, love loves beyond mind,
for in the end,
there is no end to it.

Helen of Troy had a wandering glance;
Sappho's restriction was only the sky;
Ninon was ever the chatter of France;
But oh, what a good girl am I!

Believe in God, all must but ‘why'?
Why shouldn't we trust the Creator?
Creator, who made everything;
Everything, both unseen and seen;
Seen by our two just earthly eyes;
Eyes that perceive God-made Nature;
Nature designed by God for all;
All men must therefore trust the Lord;
Lord Almighty, who made by love;
Love infinite, shouldn't we believe?

In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Who in despite of view is pleased to dote;

I wish I loved the Human Race;
I wish I loved its silly face;
I wish I liked the way it walks;
I wish I liked the way it talks;
And when I'm introduced to one
I wish I thought What Jolly Fun!

While fates permit us, let's be merry;
Pass all we must the fatal ferry;
And this our life, too, whirls away,
With the rotation of the day.

L ights of the morning gather on the hill;
I n every window, quiet hopes awake;
G uarded by the roads we choose to take,
H earts learn that freedom is a daily will;
T hrough storm and doubt, the open hands are still;
S o let the common promises not break.

F or a morning after winter,
E very window fills with flame;
B eneath the thawing branches,
R estless birds begin their game;
U nder blue, the roads awaken,
A nd the cold gives up its claim;
R each your arms toward springtime,
Y our new beginning has a name.

After the storm, the stillness;
After the wound, the skin;
After the long and sleepless night,
The morning entering in.
Leaves move across a quiet day,
A mother smiles at the door;
The smallest gentle word can make
The broken heart endure.

H appy morning, open sky;
A new year turns its face;
P ast shadows step behind the door,
P eace settles in their place;
Y outh may count another year,
D reams may still begin;
A ll the light the night concealed
R ises from within.

Love opens like a rosebud,
Delicate and new;
It carries scent and color,
And thorns to travel through.
It starts beneath the soil as faith,
Then asks for care and rain;
Neglected, it will close its leaves;
Nourished, it blooms again.

R un beyond the gray crowd;
A ppear where the bright drops fall;
I n every sprinkler, find a prism;
N ame the colors, one and all;
B ecome the rainbow’s hidden friend;
O pen your hands to rain;
W alk through the sunlit water,
And be yourself again.

J ust as the old year closes,
A new one lifts its face;
N ew hopes climb the open road,
U nburdened by the past;
A ll our resolutions shine,
R eady for the sea;
Y et the waves may change their course—
Still, we sail willingly.

Helen possessed a dangerous smile;
Sappho had the moon for a friend;
Ninon could make a whole room listen—
But I am good from beginning to end!
I never tease the heart of a stranger,
I never flirt with a glance;
I merely enter the party
And accidentally start a romance.

To me you have a winter grace,
Clear eyes and weather-colored hair;
You wear the simplest clothes with ease,
And carry sunlight everywhere.
No passing season dims your spirit;
No hard road makes you less than fair.

Courage is a lantern,
courage is a shield;
It is the quiet choice to stand
When fear would make you yield.
It may belong to knight or mouse,
To parent, child, or dove;
Its greatest work is often done
In guarding those we love.
Courage does not need a crowd;
One brave heart is enough.
